.TH AUTOSNAP 1 "2026-06-25" "autosnap" "autosnap manual"
.SH NAME
autosnap \- local checkpointing for Git worktrees
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B autosnap
.RI <command>
.RI [flags]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B autosnap
creates local, check-gated Git checkpoints while you work. It is a safety net
between intentional Git commits, not a replacement for normal commits.
.PP
By default, autosnap stores passing checkpoints as local Git refs under
.BR refs/autosnapshots/ .
It does not commit to the active branch, does not push, and does not overwrite
work during normal daemon operation.
.SH QUICK START
Run autosnap inside the Git worktree you want to protect:
.PP
.nf
autosnap config init
.fi
.PP
Edit
.B .autosnap.toml
and set the check command for your project:
.PP
.nf
check = "make test"
idle_seconds = 60
snapshot_mode = "both"
commit_mode = "checkpoint"
.fi
.PP
Then start the daemon:
.PP
.nf
autosnap start
autosnap status
.fi
.PP
After editing
.BR .autosnap.toml ,
restart the daemon:
.PP
.nf
autosnap restart
.fi
.SH NORMAL WORKFLOW
Leave autosnap running while you work. Make normal Git commits yourself when
the work is ready. Use:
.PP
.nf
autosnap pending --explain
.fi
.PP
to see whether checkpoints have been integrated by your manual commits. The
status
.B exact
means the checkpoint tree matches the current branch tip exactly.
.SH SAFETY MODEL
In default checkpoint mode, autosnap does not commit to the active branch and
does not push. Checkpoints are local refs in:
.PP
.nf
refs/autosnapshots/<branch>/<timestamp>
.fi
.PP
Commands such as
.BR pick ,
.BR restore ,
and
.B promote
are explicit recovery commands. They may modify the worktree or branch and
refuse to run on a dirty worktree and index unless
.B --force
is passed.
.SH DOCUMENTATION
Installed Markdown documentation is available under:
.PP
.nf
/usr/share/doc/autosnap/
.fi
.PP
To locate installed documentation, run:
.PP
.nf
autosnap docs
.fi
.SH COMMANDS
Common commands:
.PP
.nf
autosnap config init
autosnap start
autosnap status
autosnap logs
autosnap pending
autosnap list
autosnap show <checkpoint>
autosnap pick <checkpoint>
autosnap restore <checkpoint>
autosnap promote <checkpoint>
autosnap prune
.fi
.PP
See
.BR autosnap-<command> (1)
for generated subcommand reference pages.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1)
